  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2024.19818</id>
    <title>Repatterning Interactions in Remote Work Environments: Bolstering Psychological Safety Through Mutually Humanizing Self-Disclosure</title>
    <updated>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Vanessa M. Conzon</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Organization Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Existing research on psychological safety suggests that remote work might make it more challenging for workers to engage in the sorts of interactions that promote psychological safety. This raises the question: How is psychological safety bolstered when groups work remotely? Drawing on data from a seven-year longitudinal qualitative study—including 275 interviews and approximately 330 hours of observations—of a professional services organization that shifted from an in-person work environment to a remote work environment, I present a process model of how psychological safety can be strengthened among workers interacting virtually. I find that remote work supports interactional patterns that facilitate self-disclosure among group members. Learning more about one another, workers become mutually humanized, and psychological safety is bolstered. This psychological safety is accompanied by a set of associated practices that help people coordinate on work tasks while also sustaining flexibility in where and when they work. Reflecting improved psychological safety and associated practices, group members attend to and accomplish both work tasks and nonwork activities. This study contributes to the psychological safety literature by identifying a distinct process through which psychological safety can be promoted in remote work environments: mutual humanization via self-disclosure, which is supported through interactional patterns facilitated by remote work. Importantly, this distinct group-initiated process expands theorization beyond its current focus on leader-initiated social learning. This study also identifies how psychological safety supports role management, integrating theories of psychological safety and work-nonwork roles to expand understanding of psychological safety’s positive consequences.History: This paper has been accepted for the Organization Science Special Issue on Psychological Safety.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2024.19818"/>
    <category term="Organization Science"/>
    <published>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2023.18038</id>
    <title>Dominant Deceptions: How Future-Oriented Narratives Transform into Deception</title>
    <updated>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Tiona Zuzul, Aharon Cohen Mohliver</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Organization Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Drawing on a multiyear, qualitative study of a promising venture in a nascent industry, we develop a process model tracing how future-oriented narratives can transform into deception. In the venture that we studied, the founder’s future-oriented narratives were initially viewed as evidence of his visionary foresight. As these narratives traveled downward through the organization, employees responsible for operations translated this broad vision into specific targets and metrics. Some details described an attainable future; others were so far-fetched that they became liminal, falling into a gray zone between right and wrong. As internal and external demands for tangible progress grew, this expectations-reality gap prompted employees to engage in temporal reorientation, shifting their statements from “we will” to “we have.” Their statements crossed from liminal to deceptive, asserting as fact something that did not exist. Once formalized in presentations, websites, and other artefacts, the claims became dominant deceptions: codified, specific, past- or present-tense deceptions deliberately reproduced across the organization. In this codified form, the deceptions became misconduct that, if detected, could have been acted on by social control agents. We propose that three enabling conditions sustained this transformation: visionary attribution (belief in the founder’s unique foresight), interpretive flexibility (the ambiguity of new technologies that allows for multiple plausible meanings), and moral justification (the framing of deception as serving a higher mission). Because the process unfolded diffusely and incrementally, the transformation could only be observed retrospectively in the accumulation of actions across individuals and over time. By tracing this process, we extend research in narratives and in organizational misconduct.Funding: Funding for this research was provided by the London Business School and the Harvard Business School.Supplemental Material: The online appendix is available at https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2023.18038 .</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2023.18038"/>
    <category term="Organization Science"/>
    <published>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.1741</id>
    <title>Optimizing Input Data Collection for Ranking and Selection</title>
    <updated>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Eunhye Song, Taeho Kim</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Operations Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study a ranking and selection (R&amp;S) problem when all solutions share common parametric Bayesian input models updated with the data collected from multiple independent data-generating sources. Our objective is to identify the best system by designing a sequential sampling algorithm that collects input and simulation data given a budget. We adopt the most probable best (MPB) as the estimator of the optimum and show that its posterior probability of optimality converges to one at an exponential rate as the sampling budget increases. Assuming that the input parameters belong to a finite set, we characterize the optimal static sampling ratios for input and simulation data that maximize the convergence rate. Using these ratios as guidance, we propose the optimal sampling algorithm for R&amp;S (OSAR) that achieves the optimal ratios almost surely in the limit. We further extend OSAR by adopting the kernel ridge regression to improve the simulation output mean prediction. This not only improves OSAR’s finite-sample performance, but also lets us tackle the case where the input parameters lie in a continuous space with a strong consistency guarantee for finding the optimum. We numerically demonstrate that OSAR outperforms a state-of-the-art competitor.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2025.1741"/>
    <category term="Operations Research"/>
    <published>2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00</published>
